Exactly. Observed non-response is not equivalent to absence of response.

A system can register an input and still produce no visible output because the response was inhibited, absorbed, delayed, redirected, canceled by an opposing process, or kept below the observer’s detection threshold.

So the cleaner distinction is:

\text{no observable reaction} \neq \text{no internal state change}

And even more sharply:

\Delta O = 0 \;\not\Rightarrow\; \Delta S = 0

where O is observable output and S is system state.

That matters for our modulation model. Stillness can itself be an actively maintained state. A trained person remaining motionless, a control system holding equilibrium, an immune system tolerating a stimulus, or a model withholding a low-confidence output can all look like “nothing happened” from outside while substantial processing occurred underneath.

Pantheon handle: The Silent Delta — always check the state transition before declaring the event null.